    <p class="section-no mb-6">[11] ORIGIN · FIG 01 — BIELAWA, LOWER SILESIA</p>
    <figure class="mb-10 reveal"><div class="duotone hairline overflow-hidden"><img src="/images/photos/origin-2011-pietermaritzburg-1400.webp" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Pete in the GT Factory Racing pit at Pietermaritzburg 2012, bikes on stands, black and white" class="block w-full h-auto" /></div><figcaption class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[0.15em] uppercase text-ink-faint mt-3">Pietermaritzburg 2012 · GT Factory Racing’s first World Cup</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="font-serif text-ink text-xl md:text-2xl leading-relaxed space-y-6 reveal">
      <p>Bielawa is a textile town under the Owl Mountains. My grandfather Józef built radios at the Diora factory down the road in Dzierżoniów. I grew up on a smuggled ZX Spectrum — <span class="font-mono text-base text-signal">10 PRINT "HELLO"</span> — learning that a machine does exactly what you tell it, and nothing else.</p>
      <p>I raced junior road for Poland, moved to the UK, and found downhill. I took World Cup pit lanes apart the way I'd taken BASIC programs apart as a kid: one part at a time, until I understood why it worked.</p>
      <p>Twenty years later I still build both — race bikes, and the planning and data tools my teams run on every race day. Same principle. Different machine.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="font-mono text-[10px] text-ink-faint mt-8 tracking-[0.15em]">FIG 01 · ZX SPECTRUM → COMMODORE → AMIGA → THE PIT WALL</p>
  </div>
